---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const pageTitle = "International Size Chart Guide | US, UK, EU Size Conversion | SDF Clothing";
const pageDescription = "Convert clothing sizes between US, UK, and EU standards for men's and women's garments. Understand grading rules and how manufacturers build size charts for production.";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": pageTitle,
    "description": pageDescription,
    "author": { "@id": "https://sdfltd.com/#org" },
    "publisher": { "@id": "https://sdfltd.com/#org" },
    "url": "https://sdfltd.com/insights/international-size-chart-guide/",
    "mainEntityOfPage": "https://sdfltd.com/insights/international-size-chart-guide/"
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is a UK size 12 in US sizing?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A UK size 12 in women's clothing is typically a US size 8. UK sizes generally run 4 sizes higher than US sizes for women's garments."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is grading in clothing manufacturing?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Grading is the process of scaling a base pattern (usually a middle size like Medium) up and down to create the full size range, following fixed measurement increments between each size."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How is EU size different from US size?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "EU sizing uses a different numbering scale entirely. A US size 8 (women's) is approximately EU size 38-40, and a US men's Medium is approximately EU size 48-50, though exact conversion varies by brand and garment type."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} schema={schema}>

  <div class="container" style="padding: 3rem 1.5rem 5rem;">

    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <p style="font-size: 0.72rem; color: #FFFFFF; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase;">
        <a href="/" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">Home</a>
        <span style="margin: 0 0.5rem; color: #333;">›</span>
        <a href="/insights/" style="color: #aaa; text-decoration: none;">Insights</a>
        <span style="margin: 0 0.5rem; color: #333;">›</span>
        <span style="color: #FFFFFF;">International Size Chart Guide</span>
      </p>
    </nav>

    <article>

      <header style="max-width: 760px; margin-bottom: 3rem; padding-bottom: 2rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;">
        <span style="font-size: 0.62rem; letter-spacing: 0.25em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #e00; display: block; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Sizing Guide</span>
        <h1 style="font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.8rem); font-weight: 200; color: #fff; line-height: 1.2; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; letter-spacing: 0.01em;">
          International Size Chart Guide — US, UK & EU Conversion
        </h1>
        <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #aaa; line-height: 1.9; margin: 0;">
          A reference for converting clothing sizes between US, UK, and EU standards, and understanding how manufacturers build size charts and grading rules for production.
        </p>
      </header>

      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">Women's Size Conversion (US / UK / EU)</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">UK women's sizes generally run 4 sizes higher than US sizes for the same fit. EU sizes use a separate numbering scale entirely.</p>

      <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.88rem;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #333;">
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #e00; font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">US</th>
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #e00; font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">UK</th>
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #e00; font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">EU</th>
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #e00; font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">Letter</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">0</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">4</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">32</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">XXS</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">2</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">6</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">34</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">XS</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">4</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">8</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">36</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">S</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">6</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">10</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">38</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">S</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">8</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">12</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">40</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">M</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">10</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">14</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">42</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">M</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">12</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">16</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">44</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">L</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">14</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">18</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">46</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">XL</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">16</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">20</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">48</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">XXL</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">For plus-size specific ranges, see our <a href="/plus-size-clothing-manufacturer" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">plus size clothing manufacturer</a> page. For smaller frames, see our <a href="/petite-clothing-manufacturer/" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">petite clothing manufacturer</a> page.</p>

      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">Men's Size Conversion (US / UK / EU)</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Men's US and UK sizes are generally identical for tops (S/M/L/XL). EU sizing again uses a separate numbering scale.</p>

      <div style="overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.88rem;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #333;">
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #e00; font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">US / UK</th>
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #e00; font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">EU</th>
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #e00; font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">Chest (in)</th>
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #e00; font-weight: 400; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.05em;">Chest (cm)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">XS</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">44</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">33-34</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">84-86</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">S</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">46</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">35-37</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">89-94</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">M</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">48-50</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">38-40</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">96-101</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">L</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">52</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">41-43</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">104-109</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">XL</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">54</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">44-46</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">112-117</td></tr>
            <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid #1a1a1a;"><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">XXL</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">56-58</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">47-49</td><td style="padding: 0.75rem 1rem; color: #ccc;">119-124</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">Measurements above are chest circumference guides for standard fit tops. Actual measurements vary by brand, cut, and garment category — always confirm exact spec against your tech pack rather than relying on size labels alone.</p>

      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">What Is Grading?</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Grading is the process of scaling a base pattern — usually a middle size like Medium — up and down to produce the full size range for a style. Rather than drafting each size from scratch, the pattern maker applies fixed measurement increments (the "grade rule") at each cutting point: chest, waist, hip, sleeve length, and so on.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">A typical grade rule for a t-shirt might add 2 inches to the chest and 0.5 inches to the sleeve length between each size. These increments are documented in the grading specification, part of a complete <a href="/how-to-create-a-tech-pack" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">tech pack</a>.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">Consistent grading across your size range matters for fit consistency — customers who buy a Medium in one style should get a comparable fit in Medium across your other styles. Poor grading is one of the most common causes of size-related returns.</p>

      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">Building a Size Chart for Production</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">When you send a size chart to a manufacturer, include measurement points specific to the garment type — for example, a t-shirt spec sheet needs chest, body length, shoulder width, sleeve length, and neck opening, measured flat (garment laid flat, not around the body).</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">Specify tolerance for each measurement point — typically ±0.5 to ±1 cm for most points, tighter for critical measurements like neck opening. This tolerance is what quality control checks against during inspection.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">If you don't have an existing size chart, request a manufacturer's standard block pattern measurements as a starting point, then adjust based on your target market and fit preference (slim, regular, or relaxed).</p>

      <h2 style="font-size: 0.78rem; font-weight: 400; margin: 0 0 1.25rem; color: #fff; letter-spacing: 0.05em; text-transform: uppercase;">Size Chart FAQ</h2>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"><strong>What is a UK size 12 in US sizing?</strong> A UK size 12 in women's clothing is typically a US size 8. UK sizes generally run 4 sizes higher than US sizes for women's garments.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"><strong>What is grading in clothing manufacturing?</strong> Grading is the process of scaling a base pattern up and down to create the full size range, following fixed measurement increments between each size.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"><strong>How is EU size different from US size?</strong> EU sizing uses a different numbering scale entirely. A US size 8 (women's) is approximately EU size 38-40, and a US men's Medium is approximately EU size 48-50, though exact conversion varies by brand and garment type.</p>
      <p style="color: #aaa; line-height: 1.8; font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;"><strong>Do I need a different size chart for each garment type?</strong> Yes. A t-shirt, a pair of trousers, and a jacket each have different measurement points and fit conventions, so each garment type needs its own size chart, even within the same brand and size range.</p>

      <div style="text-align: center; padding: 2.5rem; border: 1px solid #1a1a1a; border-top: 0.6px solid #e00; background: #050505; margin-top: 3rem;">
        <p style="color: #fff; font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-weight: 300;">
          Need help building a size chart or grading spec for your first production run? SDF Clothing provides grading support as part of tech pack development.
        </p>
        <a href="/contact" style="display: inline-block; background: #e00; color: #fff; padding: 0.9rem 2.5rem; text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.15em; text-transform: uppercase; transition: background 0.2s;">
          Discuss Your Size Chart
        </a>
      </div>

      <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: #aaa; margin-top: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.8;">
        Read our <a href="/inclusive-sizing-guide" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">inclusive sizing guide</a> for extended-range considerations, or learn <a href="/how-to-create-a-tech-pack" style="color: #e00; text-decoration: none;">how to create a tech pack</a> for full spec documentation.
      </p>

    </article>

  </div>

</BaseLayout>
